      mostSupport: `
<p>In August 1778, a man named Haym Salomon wrote a letter to Congress. He had just escaped from New York. The British army held the city. He wrote that he had lost all he owned. He had left his wife and a tiny baby behind. He asked Congress for one thing, a job.</p>
<h3>A Prisoner in New York</h3>
<p>Salomon was born in Poland in 1740. His family was Jewish. He learned many languages. Then he moved to New York. He worked as a <strong>merchant</strong>. A merchant buys and sells goods.</p>
<p>In 1776 the British took New York. They said Salomon was a spy. They put him in prison. But a Hessian general needed a man who spoke many languages. Hessians were German soldiers paid to fight for Britain. The general let Salomon out to work for him. Salomon bought food and goods for the officers.</p>
<p>The job let him move around the city. So he helped American and French prisoners. He gave them money. He helped them escape. In 1778 the British came after him. He fled to Philadelphia.</p>
<h3>Working for Robert Morris</h3>
<p>Congress did not give him a job. So Salomon became a <strong>broker</strong>. A broker helps people buy and sell things. He keeps a small fee. In 1781 Robert Morris took charge of the country's money. The government had almost no cash. France had lent money to America. But the loan came as paper notes. Someone had to sell the notes for cash. Morris gave that job to Salomon. He sold about $200,000 worth of notes. He was paid a fee for each sale.</p>
<p>Salomon also lent his own money to some men in Congress. One was James Madison. Those were loans to the men. They were not loans to the government.</p>
<h3>The Legend</h3>
<p>Salomon died in January 1785. He was about 44. Years later, his son went to Congress. He said his father had lent the government a lot of money. He asked for $353,000. Later he asked for $3,000,000. Congress never paid. But the story spread. In 1975 a stamp called Salomon a "Financial Hero." It said he raised most of the money for the war.</p>
<p>Historians checked the records. They found no loans from Salomon to the government. He never said he made any. His own letter in 1778 asked only for a job.</p>`,
