      challenge: `
<p>On the morning of April 19, 1775, the minutemen of Framingham, Massachusetts, marched toward Concord. Word had come that British soldiers were on the road from Boston. One of the men in Captain Simon Edgell's company was Peter Salem. A few weeks earlier he had been enslaved, and the man who had freed him, Major Lawson Buckminster, commanded the Framingham minutemen.</p>
<h3>Freed to Enlist</h3>
<p>Salem was born into slavery around 1750 in Framingham, where his mother was also enslaved. His first enslaver was Jeremiah Belknap, who sold him early in 1775 to Buckminster. Buckminster commanded Framingham's <strong>minutemen</strong>, militia who promised to be ready to fight at a minute's notice, and he freed Salem so that Salem could enlist. A Massachusetts historian of the 1800s, Emory Washburn, wrote that letting an enslaved man enlist "worked a practical emancipation." Framingham's town historian wrote that Belknap and Buckminster later gave the town a bond promising to support Salem for the rest of his life. No one knows for sure where the name Salem came from. It was not the name of either family that had owned him.</p>
<h3>The Redoubt on Breed's Hill</h3>
<p>After Lexington and Concord, the militia gathered around Boston, and Salem joined Captain Thomas Drury's company in Colonel John Nixon's regiment. On the night of June 16, 1775, American soldiers dug a <strong>redoubt</strong>, a small fort of packed earth, on Breed's Hill above Charlestown. The next day about 2,200 British soldiers attacked it. The fight is known as the Battle of Bunker Hill. The Americans drove back two attacks, but each man had only about thirteen cartridges, and when the powder ran out the British took the hill on their third try. The British won the hill and lost 1,054 men killed or wounded, the heaviest British losses of any battle in the war.</p>
<p>Major John Pitcairn of the British marines was killed in the attack on the redoubt. In 1818 Samuel Swett wrote a history of the battle that said "a black soldier named Salem shot him through, and he fell." In a later edition Swett added a story that soldiers collected money for Salem and presented him to General Washington. Swett wrote more than forty years after the battle. Other men claimed the shot, and in 1787 Jeremy Belknap, a minister and historian, wrote that a Black man from Groton shot him. The only count of the wounds comes from an American clergyman, John Eliot, who wrote that four balls hit Pitcairn. Historians cannot say who fired the shot that killed him, and it is possible that several men did. Another Black soldier, Salem Poor, fought at Bunker Hill that day, and on December 5, 1775, a group of officers signed a <strong>petition</strong>, a written request to the government, calling him a brave and gallant soldier. The two men are sometimes confused.</p>
<h3>Five Years a Soldier</h3>
<p>Salem reenlisted again and again. In 1776 he served in the 4th Continental Regiment and fought at Harlem Heights and Trenton. On January 1, 1777, he signed up for three more years in the 6th Massachusetts Regiment. He was at Saratoga in 1777, at Monmouth in 1778 and at Stony Point in 1779, where a light infantry company stormed a British fort on the Hudson River at night under General Anthony Wayne. He was discharged on December 31, 1779, enlisted once more, and ended his service on March 1, 1780, almost five years after he marched to Concord.</p>
<h3>A Cabin in Leicester</h3>
<p>In September 1783 Salem married Katy Benson. They had no children, and by 1790 they were no longer living together. He settled in Leicester, Massachusetts, where he built a cabin and earned a living weaving cane seats for chairs, making baskets and mending chairs. He grew old poor. He died in Framingham on August 16, 1816, and the town buried him in a <strong>pauper's grave</strong>, a grave paid for by the town because he had no money. Sources differ on whether he died in the town poorhouse or at a neighbor's house.</p>
<p>In 1855 William C. Nell, a Black historian, printed Swett's account in The Colored Patriots of the American Revolution. In a footnote Nell added that in some early engravings of the battle a Black soldier stood in a prominent place, but that in newer editions his figure was missing. Nell called it "a significant, but inglorious omission." In 1882 the Town of Framingham spent $150 on a granite monument for Salem's grave. The stone reads "A Soldier of the Revolution" and "Erected by the Town, 1882."</p>`
