      challenge: `
<p>On March 31, 1776, Abigail Adams wrote a letter from the family farm in Braintree, Massachusetts. Her husband, John Adams, was in Philadelphia as a delegate to the Continental Congress. The British army had sailed out of Boston two weeks before, ending a siege that had lasted since April 1775. In the letter she asked whether Congress had "declared an independancy." Then she told him what she wanted in the new nation's laws.</p>
<h3>A Minister's Daughter</h3>
<p>Abigail Smith was born on November 22, 1744, in Weymouth, Massachusetts. Her father, William Smith, was the town's Congregational minister. New England schools of that time were for boys, so she never went to school. She learned at home, reading the books in her father's library, and her spelling stayed her own for the rest of her life, because English spelling was not yet standard and she had taught herself. On October 25, 1764, a month before her twentieth birthday, she married John Adams, a young lawyer from Braintree.</p>
<h3>Running the Farm</h3>
<p>In 1774 John Adams went to Philadelphia as a delegate to the First Continental Congress. For most of the next ten years he was away, first in Congress and later in Europe as a <strong>diplomat</strong>, an official who represents his country abroad. Abigail Adams stayed in Braintree, raised their four children, and ran the farm and the family's money. She kept the family's accounts and reported to John on prices, taxes and the harvest.</p>
<p>The war came close to her home. On June 17, 1775, she heard the cannon of the Battle of Bunker Hill from Braintree. The next day she wrote to John: "The Day; perhaps the decisive Day is come on which the fate of America depends." She told him that their friend Dr. Joseph Warren had been killed, that "Charlstown is laid in ashes," and that "the constant roar of the cannon is so distressing that we can not Eat, Drink or Sleep." Their son John Quincy Adams, then seven years old, later recalled that he saw the fires with his own eyes.</p>
<p>The two wrote to each other constantly. The Massachusetts Historical Society holds 1,160 letters between them, written from 1762 to 1801, and the family saved them. They are one of the best records historians have of family life during the Revolution.</p>
<h3>Remember the Ladies</h3>
<p>In her letter of March 31, 1776, she asked John to keep women in mind when Congress wrote the laws of the new country. "I desire you would Remember the Ladies, and be more generous and favourable to them than your ancestors," she wrote. "Do not put such unlimited power into the hands of the Husbands. Remember all Men would be tyrants if they could." She warned that if the laws ignored women, "we are determined to foment a Rebelion, and will not hold ourselves bound by any Laws in which we have no voice, or Representation." The Patriots had made the same argument against Britain, that Parliament could not tax people who had no <strong>representation</strong>, no one to speak for them in the government. Under the laws of the time, a married woman's property and earnings were controlled by her husband, and no woman in Massachusetts could vote.</p>
<p>John Adams answered on April 14. "As to your extraordinary Code of Laws, I cannot but laugh," he wrote. He said he had heard that the war had loosened every kind of authority, that "Indians slighted their Guardians and Negroes grew insolent to their Masters," but that her letter was the first hint "that another Tribe more numerous and powerfull than all the rest were grown discontented." He joked that giving up the men's authority would subject them "to the Despotism of the Peticoat," and he added, "you are so saucy, I wont blot it out." That summer the Declaration of Independence said nothing about women's rights.</p>
<h3>Against Slavery</h3>
<p>Adams also wrote against slavery. In September 1774 she told John that it had "allways appeard a most iniquitious Scheme to me" to fight for liberty while "daily robbing and plundering" it from people who had as good a right to it. In February 1797 a free Black boy named James began attending Master Heath's evening school in Quincy. A neighbor named Faxon objected to his being there. Adams went to Faxon and defended the boy. "The Boy is a Freeman as much as any of the young Men," she wrote on February 13, "and merely because his Face is Black, is he to be denied instruction."</p>
<h3>First Lady</h3>
<p>John Adams became the second president of the United States in 1797, and Abigail Adams was the <strong>First Lady</strong>, the wife of the president, from 1797 to 1801. In later years, women who campaigned for the right to vote quoted her 1776 letter in their own speeches and books. Abigail Adams died of typhoid fever at home in Quincy on October 28, 1818, at the age of 73.</p>`
