      challenge: `
<p>On March 26, 1772, readers of the Massachusetts Spy, a Boston newspaper, found nearly half of page three filled with scenes from a play. The play was set in a faraway country called Upper Servia, ruled by a greedy official named Rapatio. No author's name was printed, and a second installment followed on April 23. Readers in Boston knew who Rapatio stood for. He was Thomas Hutchinson, the royal governor of Massachusetts. The play was called The Adulateur. The author was a 43-year-old mother of five from Plymouth named Mercy Otis Warren.</p>
<h3>Learning With Her Brothers</h3>
<p>Mercy Otis was born on September 25, 1728, in Barnstable, Massachusetts, the daughter of a farmer, lawyer and judge who served in the colonial legislature. Girls then could not attend college, and most received little schooling. Her father let her study with her brothers under their uncle, the Reverend Jonathan Russell, who was their <strong>tutor</strong>, a teacher who taught them at home and prepared the boys for Harvard. Her brother James Otis Jr. became a lawyer. In 1761 he argued in court against the writs of assistance, papers that let British customs officers search any ship or house for smuggled goods. In 1764 he published a pamphlet, The Rights of the British Colonies Asserted and Proved, which argued that Parliament could not tax the colonists without their consent. In 1769 a customs official struck him on the head during a fight in a Boston coffeehouse, and he never fully recovered.</p>
<h3>Plays Against the Governor</h3>
<p>In 1754 Mercy married James Warren, a merchant and farmer from Plymouth who had been a friend of her brother at Harvard. He later served as speaker of the Massachusetts House and as president of the Provincial Congress. They had five sons. Their house on North Street in Plymouth became a meeting place for Patriots, including John Adams and Samuel Adams. In the 1770s Warren began writing <strong>satire</strong>, writing that uses humor and exaggeration to attack people in power. Her plays The Adulateur (1772), The Defeat (1773) and The Group (1775) were printed in the Massachusetts Spy and the Boston Gazette and as pamphlets. They were <strong>closet dramas</strong>, plays meant to be read rather than performed, and Massachusetts had outlawed stage plays in any case. They showed Hutchinson and his supporters as greedy men who sold out their country for the king's favor.</p>
<p>Warren published the plays <strong>anonymously</strong>, without her name. At the time most people believed politics was not a woman's business, and a woman who wrote about it in public risked her reputation. Patriot leaders knew who wrote the plays. John Adams praised her writing and sent her news from Congress, and she became a close friend of Abigail Adams. The two women exchanged letters for forty years.</p>
<h3>The Constitution and the History</h3>
<p>After the war Warren kept writing. In 1788, as the states debated whether to approve the new Constitution, she published a nineteen-page pamphlet called Observations on the New Constitution under the name "A Columbian Patriot." It argued that the Constitution gave the national government too much power, allowed a standing army, and had no bill of rights. For more than a century, readers believed Elbridge Gerry of Massachusetts had written it. Historians settled the question in the 1930s from Warren's own letters.</p>
<p>In 1805 Warren published History of the Rise, Progress and Termination of the American Revolution, in three volumes. Hannah Adams had published a shorter account of the war in 1799, and Warren's was the first full history of the war written by a woman. In its opening address she answered those who held that "all political attentions lay out of the road of female life," and she admitted that "the hand often shrunk back from the task; yet ... the work was not relinquished." The book also described John Adams, by then a former president, as a man whose prejudices and passions had sometimes been too strong for his judgment. In July and August 1807 Adams sent her ten angry letters, and the two stopped writing to each other. Their friend Elbridge Gerry helped bring them back together in 1812. Mercy Otis Warren died in Plymouth on October 19, 1814, at the age of 86.</p>`
