<p>The woman Martin saw was one of thousands who followed the Continental Army. Most were soldiers' wives. The army called them <strong>camp followers</strong>, civilians who traveled with an army and did its daily work. They cooked, washed clothes, nursed the sick and carried water. Gun crews needed water too, to swab out the hot barrel of a cannon between shots. No record from the battle says who carried water at Monmouth that day.</p>
