<p>In May 1780 the British army captured Charleston, South Carolina, and soon afterward a young enslaved man named Boston King walked into the city and offered to work for the British. He was about twenty years old and had grown up on Richard Waring's plantation near Charleston, where his mother worked as a nurse and seamstress and his father had been taken from Africa as a young man. At sixteen King had been sent to a carpenter in a nearby town to learn the trade, and the carpenter beat him "without mercy." Waring sided with the rebels when the war began, and when the British took the city, King ran to them. He later wrote that the British "received me readily" and that he "began to feel the happiness of liberty."</p>
